From 45d71875c112bab11a5c276bfb60f6d86c296d7b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 05:00:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: with Dolby Vision and HDR10+ in one video, a
 setting says which is sent

A video whose packets carry both Dolby Vision (its RPU and, in profile 7, an enhancement layer)
and HDR10+ metadata of its own (SMPTE ST 2094-40) went out as Dolby Vision wherever native output
could be had: the demuxer never looked at a Dolby Vision stream for HDR10+, and the HDR10+
InfoFrames of its pictures went out only on the ordinary output. On a display that takes both
(TCL, Hisense, Panasonic and Philips among them), which of the two looks better is for the user to
say.

A new setting, System > Display, "With Dolby Vision and HDR10+ in one video"
(videoscreen.hadesdualhdr): Dolby Vision, the default, which is the same path and the same bytes as
before, or HDR10+. The demuxer looks for HDR10+ metadata in the first 48 packets of a stream with
Dolby Vision of its own (CDVDDemuxFFmpeg::ProbeDualHdr10Plus(), with CDVHdr10Plus::Read()); where it
finds them, DVBRIDGE::DualHdr10PlusFound() decides by DVBRIDGE::DualVerdictOf() and says in kodi.log
what the video is sent with and why, also with the default. HDR10+ only where the base layer is
HDR10 (profile 7 of a disc, and 8.1), where the display takes native Dolby Vision and HDR10+ can be
sent to it (its block, and the connector's HDR10PLUS_VSIF), and where the output chosen for the
video is the automatic one or HDR10+. The stream then says so (CDVDStreamInfo::doviHdr10PlusWins),
the player opens it again as for a new file, native output and the reconstruction leave it aside,
and it goes out on the ordinary output: its base layer with its own HDR10+ InfoFrame, picture by
picture, as before for such a video on a display without Dolby Vision.

For one video, "Dolby Vision output (this video)" offers, for such a video on such a display, its
own Dolby Vision and its own HDR10+ (OutputChoice::DOLBY_VISION, and a new OutputChoice::HDR10PLUS,
which plays any other video as the automatic choice does), kept for the file as the other choices
are. The Dolby Vision page of the player information says why native output is left aside.

Limits: the HDR10+ metadata are known from the packets only, after the stream opened: with HDR10+
chosen, the mode of the video was chosen for Dolby Vision, and the display may change its signal
once more at the start (not measured). None of it has run on a display.

The idea is p3i's (CoreELEC, dual.priority). Strings in en_gb, es_es, es_ar and es_mx. Host test:
dualhdr_test, 51 checks, also with ASan and UBSan, and 12 mutations of the rule, each of which makes
it fail.
